# Chapter 2. Introduction to Neural Networks

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:nx, "1.0.0"},
  {:kino, "0.19.1"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "0.1.13"}
])

# Shared presentation only; the chapter's table contents are unchanged.
defmodule Chapter02.Tables do
  @moduledoc false

  @type column :: atom() | String.t()
  @type cell :: atom() | number() | String.t()
  @type row :: %{optional(column()) => cell()}

  @spec tabs([row()], [column()], String.t()) :: Kino.Layout.t()
  @spec tabs([row()], [column()], String.t(), String.t() | nil) :: Kino.Layout.t()
  def tabs(rows, columns, filename, markdown_source \\ nil) do
    markdown_source = markdown_source || markdown(rows, columns)
    csv_source = csv(rows, columns)

    Kino.Layout.tabs([
      {"Interactive", Kino.DataTable.new(rows, keys: columns)},
      {"Markdown", Kino.Markdown.new(markdown_source)},
      {"CSV",
       Kino.Layout.grid([
         Kino.Download.new(fn -> csv_source end, filename: filename, label: "Download CSV"),
         Kino.Text.new(csv_source)
       ])}
    ])
  end

  @spec from_markdown(String.t(), String.t()) :: Kino.Layout.t()
  def from_markdown(markdown_source, filename) do
    [header, _separator | lines] =
      markdown_source |> String.trim() |> String.split("\n", trim: true)

    columns = split_row(header)

    rows =
      Enum.map(lines, fn line ->
        values = split_row(line)

        unless length(values) == length(columns) do
          raise ArgumentError, "table row does not match its header"
        end

        Map.new(Enum.zip(columns, values))
      end)

    tabs(rows, columns, filename, markdown_source)
  end

  @spec split_row(String.t()) :: [String.t()]
  defp split_row(line) do
    line
    |> String.trim()
    |> String.trim_leading("|")
    |> String.trim_trailing("|")
    |> String.split("|")
    |> Enum.map(&String.trim/1)
  end

  @spec markdown([row()], [column()]) :: String.t()
  defp markdown(rows, columns) do
    header = markdown_row(columns)
    separator = markdown_row(Enum.map(columns, fn _ -> "---" end))

    body =
      Enum.map(rows, fn row ->
        markdown_row(Enum.map(columns, &Map.fetch!(row, &1)))
      end)

    Enum.join([header, separator | body], "\n") <> "\n"
  end

  @spec markdown_row([cell()]) :: String.t()
  defp markdown_row(values) do
    "| " <> Enum.map_join(values, " | ", &markdown_cell/1) <> " |"
  end

  @spec markdown_cell(cell()) :: String.t()
  defp markdown_cell(value) do
    value
    |> to_string()
    |> String.replace("\\", "\\\\")
    |> String.replace("|", "\\|")
    |> String.replace("\r\n", "<br>")
    |> String.replace(["\r", "\n"], "<br>")
  end

  @spec csv([row()], [column()]) :: String.t()
  defp csv(rows, columns) do
    records = [columns | Enum.map(rows, fn row -> Enum.map(columns, &Map.fetch!(row, &1)) end)]

    Enum.map_join(records, "\r\n", fn record ->
      Enum.map_join(record, ",", &csv_field/1)
    end) <> "\r\n"
  end

  @spec csv_field(cell()) :: String.t()
  defp csv_field(value) do
    "\"" <> String.replace(to_string(value), "\"", "\"\"") <> "\""
  end
end
```

## Reader's guide

**Neuroevolution through Elixir**  
Part I - Foundations

**The central idea:** a neural network transforms signals. Recurrent state lets its
past affect its present. Plasticity changes how it will process future signals.
Evolution can search over all three: computation, memory, and adaptation.

This chapter remasters Gene I. Sher's *Handbook of Neuroevolution Through Erlang*
(2013), Chapter 2, printed pages 43-79. The original section numbers and conceptual
sequence are retained. Source notes refer to that chapter as **[S]**.

The explanations condense Sher's text. All Elixir/Nx implementations, executable
experiments, new diagrams, exercises, and the Generation-Evaluation-Selection
(GES) connection are **remaster additions**, not code or claims attributed to
Sher. Numerical discrepancies and terminology qualifications are identified where
they matter. This is a study adaptation, not an official revised edition.

### Chapter map

| Section                                              | Question                                                | Main representation                        |
| ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| 2.1 Biological Neural Network                        | What does the biological analogy contribute?            | Prose, table, timing example               |
| 2.2 Artificial Neural Network                        | What do we retain in an artificial neuron?              | Equation and diagram                       |
| 2.2.1 The Neurode in Detail                          | How do weights, bias, and activation produce an output? | Elixir, Nx, activation plot, XOR audit     |
| 2.3 Neural Networks and Neural Network Based Systems | How do neurons form circuits and agents?                | Tensor shapes and topology                 |
| 2.3.1 Recurrent Neural Networks and Memory Loops     | How can a fixed network remember?                       | Recurrent rollout and plot                 |
| 2.3.2 A Neural Network Based System                  | How does a network interact with a world?               | Sensor-controller-actuator example         |
| 2.4 Learning Vs. Training                            | What changes, when, and under whose control?            | Comparison table                           |
| 2.5 Supervised "Learning" Algorithms                 | How can labeled examples set weights?                   | Backpropagation with Nx                    |
| 2.6 Unsupervised Learning Algorithms                 | How can activity drive adaptation?                      | Hebbian, Oja, modulation, competition, SOM |
| 2.7 Summary                                          | What should we carry into neuroevolution?               | Checks, exercises, synthesis               |
| 2.8 References                                       | Which parts come from which sources?                    | Source map and editorial notes             |

